+## Result, in one paragraph
+Joint formation of a unit `U` given its outside records is defined as the unique positive law on `U` whose one-site conditionals given all neighbours are the rule — the Gibbs conditional; uniqueness is the telescoping-ratio lemma, re-proved (U1). Sequential formation along an order reproduces the joint law **iff** no site records an inside neighbour together with a second recorded neighbour, inside or outside (U2); the "only if" uses the exact lemma `K_k(b,…,b) − K_k(−b,b,…,b) = ((p−q)/Z_1)[(p/Z_1)^{k−1} − (q/Z_1)^{k−1}]` (with an orthogonal form for `p = q`) and U1. Consequences: in isolation the star's sequential law equals its joint law iff at most one leaf precedes the center (`TV = 0, 0, 1/72, 5/144, 505/10368, 575/10368, 103375/1492992` for `k = 0..6`), so the campaign's expected star witness fails in isolation, while the isolated plaquette differs under every order (`455/31176`, `37/1299`) and from every convex combination (U3); in a recorded environment every connected unit with at least two sites differs from its joint law under every order, because the last site records all six neighbours (U4; the star's center-first law in the all-`+x` environment is at distance `0.403`). Clause reading: "for each site" is the sequential reading; "for each admissible set" with the whole window as the set is the static reading; intermediate units in an environment never coincide with sequential formation (U5). Recorded, not adopted.
